#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120732} :activity/id #uuid "d014fcfb-88d3-58d4-928d-09436eaced72" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121056 :tutorial/content "There is something interesting about the bases of a vector space, summarized in the theorem below:\n\nIf the vector space $V$ has a basis with $n$ vectors, then any basis of $V$ has exactly $n$ vectors.\n\nThe number $n$ of vectors in any basis of $V$ is called the **dimension of the space** $V$ and is denoted $\\textrm{dim}(V).$\n\nFor example, consider the linear span below:\n![](images/3f/3f4139a1701c74e72ca55b662b1d5c06b02f1d3b6cab9a96b4667439dc196068.svg)\n\nWe know that, in order to find a basis of the span, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $3$rd columns. This tells us that\n$\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is a basis of $H.$\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, *any basis* of $H$ will also have *exactly* two vectors. So, we have $\\textrm{dim}(H)=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6700cc12-77d9-59b2-8ee8-826575be0c12" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3517 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f0eb1a4d-9c4f-513f-be21-c3f099b4b267" :step/math-academy-id 19970 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120762}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119659 :question/id #uuid "b36a943c-f00a-5436-9256-0725a0008fdb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5410"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049222}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410546}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "c96304c7-f6d5-5dfd-b33f-9c76165825dd" :step/math-academy-id 19971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119660 :question/id #uuid "ef028d89-e645-5ef3-a881-092292e2a271" :question/math-academy-id "e-5411"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410548}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "aa44487c-2db5-5c3c-bc3c-c62ca070f704" :step/math-academy-id 19972 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120735}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121057 :tutorial/content "We can build some geometric intuition for finite-dimensional vector spaces. To illustrate, let's find the dimensions of $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\},$ $\\mathbb{R},$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nFirst, note that $\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ and $\\mathbb{R}$ can be written as $\\mathbb{R}^1.$ We have the following standard bases:\n\n![](images/44/44f00659592bc6ac2742af0105fa493efa93e58e8dd63ee9af56a2d90b0e513a.svg)\n\nConsequently, we have the following dimensions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}({R}^{0})=0,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{1})=1,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2,\\,\\text{dim}({R}^{3})=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's build some geometric intuition. We can represent the standard bases in the following diagram:\n\n![](images/16/169b5597d89fa32fd09772593cc2a9a330ee12881281dea4e3e43dc6165b9ebd.png)\n\nSo, the sequence $\\mathbb{R}^0,$ $\\mathbb{R}^1,$ $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ $\\ldots$ can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/37/379823eca26d43337744a8f4a3872e67ac537a6c193d55e39b5d6761f258f6f5.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^0$ is represented geometrically as a $0$-dimensional point.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^1$ is represented geometrically as a $1$-dimensional line segment.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is represented geometrically as a $2$-dimensional square.\n\n- The basis for $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is represented geometrically as a $3$-dimensional cube.\n\nUnfortunately, we can't visualize dimensions greater than $3,$ but we know that $\\mathbb{R}^n$ has the following standard basis consisting of $n$ vectors:\n\n![](images/c8/c8a606f297f86b56224cb9f50ba68b826e614d4f075b396a4104e19bc77c46e7.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n) = n.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "88c2cca4-9e7b-5ace-b239-798bcab0d26a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3518 :tutorial/title "Geometric Intuition for Finite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "c692fd19-bc87-5c2c-a172-5d1d8ce64f90" :step/math-academy-id 19974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121069 :tutorial/content "As defined earlier, the dimension of a vector space $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$.\n\nHowever, note that the definition above is only applicable if a vector space has at least one basis with a finite number of elements.\n\n- If a vector space has a basis with a finite number of vectors, then it is called **finite-dimensional**.\n\n- Otherwise, if there is no finite basis, the vector space is called **infinite-dimensional**.\n\nIn linear algebra, we mostly consider finite-dimensional vector spaces." :tutorial/id #uuid "e433abf5-e941-5e63-9642-c3f90b4716a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4226 :tutorial/title "Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "1510c9cb-5dac-5d6c-994f-d10e8a6d47b7" :step/math-academy-id 25615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120733}}] :activity/title "The Dimension of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120056}]}
